{"text":"Community composting can reduce the amount of food waste sent to landfill and provide material that helps improve soil. The results depend on what residents put into the collection bins and how the compost is managed. Packaging labelled compostable may still be unsuitable for a small local system.

A successful scheme requires clear instructions, regular maintenance, and a way to deal with contamination. Volunteers can support these tasks, although relying on a small number of people may make the scheme difficult to sustain. Odours and pests can also become a problem if food scraps are stored or processed poorly.

Before expanding a programme, organisers should examine participation, operating costs, and the quality of the finished compost. A high volume of collected material alone does not show that the programme is working well. Residents should be told which materials are accepted and where to report problems.","context":{},"editing_guides":[]}
